WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.994
A cryptographic inventory is the foundation for harvest-now, decrypt-later mitigation.

2
00:00:05.994 --> 00:00:16.036
It records where cryptography is used, what it protects, who owns it, which algorithms and protocols are involved, and what dependencies affect change.

3
00:00:16.036 --> 00:00:33.629
Inventory should include certificates, TLS endpoints, VPNs, SSH, secure email, code signing, firmware signing, key management systems, hardware security modules, cloud services, libraries, protocols, third-party products, and vendor-managed capabilities.

4
00:00:33.629 --> 00:00:36.898
A useful record goes beyond naming the algorithm.

5
00:00:36.898 --> 00:00:53.557
It should document the business owner, technical owner, system purpose, data protected, data lifetime, exposure, key location, certificate authority, library version, platform, protocol, vendor dependency, migration status, and known blockers.

6
00:00:53.557 --> 00:00:57.060
This context turns inventory into decision support.

7
00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:06.245
It helps teams see which systems can be tested quickly and which depend on a vendor roadmap, platform upgrade, or client compatibility work.

8
00:01:06.245 --> 00:01:09.670
Dependency mapping is where many surprises appear.

9
00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.940
A web service may terminate TLS at a load balancer.

10
00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:16.754
A mobile app may pin a certificate or use an older TLS stack.

11
00:01:16.754 --> 00:01:21.113
A backup product may rely on a vendor-managed encryption feature.

12
00:01:21.113 --> 00:01:25.784
A hardware security module may support some algorithms but not others.

13
00:01:25.784 --> 00:01:30.844
A cloud service may hide implementation details behind a managed interface.

14
00:01:30.844 --> 00:01:34.736
Each dependency affects how quickly a team can reduce risk.

15
00:01:34.736 --> 00:01:38.551
Inventory should be treated as a living program artifact.

16
00:01:38.551 --> 00:01:52.718
Initial discovery can come from scans, certificate management systems, cloud inventories, configuration repositories, endpoint management, network diagrams, application owners, and vendor questionnaires.

17
00:01:52.718 --> 00:01:56.766
The inventory will be incomplete at first, and that is normal.

18
00:01:56.766 --> 00:02:07.664
What matters is a repeatable process that improves coverage, tracks ownership, and connects cryptographic dependencies to long-term confidentiality decisions.
